---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "NordicTrack C 1650 Treadmill Review (2026): Is It Worth It?";
const description =
  "We spent four weeks walking and jogging on the NordicTrack C 1650, the longer-decked step up in NordicTrack's C-Series. Here's an honest look at whether the extra deck length and bigger screen are worth the price jump.";

const PRODUCT = {
  name: "NordicTrack C 1650",
  rating: 4.5,
  reviewCount: 2210,
  amazonUrl:
    "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=NordicTrack+C+1650+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
  priceRange: "$1,299-$1,499",
};

const SPECS = [
  { label: "Motor", value: "3.0 CHP continuous-duty" },
  { label: "Running Surface", value: "20 x 60 inches" },
  { label: "Incline", value: "0% to 10%, automatic" },
  { label: "Top Speed", value: "10 mph" },
  { label: "Display", value: "10-inch touchscreen" },
  { label: "Cushioning", value: "Standard FlexSelect cushioning" },
  { label: "Weight Capacity", value: "300 lbs" },
  { label: "Folding", value: "Yes, SpaceSaver design with EasyLift assist" },
  { label: "Folded Footprint", value: "35 x 76 inches (standing)" },
  { label: "Speakers", value: "2 x 2-inch built-in Bluetooth" },
  { label: "App / Ecosystem", value: "iFit (1-year membership included)" },
  { label: "Warranty", value: "10-yr frame, 2-yr parts, 1-yr labor" },
];

const COMPARISON = [
  {
    name: "NordicTrack C 1650",
    motor: "3.0 CHP",
    deck: "20 x 60 in.",
    incline: "0% to 10%",
    price: "$1,299-$1,499",
    highlight: true,
  },
  {
    name: "NordicTrack C 990",
    motor: "3.0 CHP",
    deck: "20 x 55 in.",
    incline: "0% to 10%",
    price: "$999-$1,099",
    highlight: false,
  },
  {
    name: "NordicTrack Commercial 1750",
    motor: "3.75 CHP",
    deck: "22 x 60 in.",
    incline: "-3% to 12%",
    price: "$1,799-$1,999",
    highlight: false,
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Commercial Treadmills",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/commercial-treadmills",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "NordicTrack C 1650 Review",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/nordictrack-c-1650-treadmill",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How does the C 1650 compare to the C 990?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "They share the same 3.0 CHP motor and 0-10% incline range, but the C 1650 has a longer 20 x 60-inch deck versus the C 990's 20 x 55-inch deck, and a larger 10-inch touchscreen instead of 7 inches. For roughly $300 more, the C 1650 is the better choice for anyone who runs regularly rather than only walks.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the C 1650 good for running, or just walking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It handles walking, incline walking, and moderate jogging comfortably thanks to its 60-inch deck length, which matches NordicTrack's Commercial-series machines. The 3.0 CHP motor is still a step below the Commercial line's 3.75 CHP, so serious daily runners logging faster-paced miles will feel more headroom on a Commercial 1750, but casual and moderate runners will be well served here.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I buy the C 1650 or save more for the Commercial 1750?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The Commercial 1750 has a stronger 3.75 CHP motor, a wider 22-inch deck, and a larger touchscreen for roughly $500 more. If your budget realistically stops at the C 1650, you're still getting a 60-inch deck length and a genuine NordicTrack and iFit experience; the Commercial 1750 is worth the jump specifically for runners who want more width and motor headroom.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I have to pay for iFit to use the C 1650?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The treadmill runs without an active subscription, but automatic incline adjustments during trainer-led workouts and the full workout library require iFit. It ships with a one-year membership included, giving you time to decide whether the coached format is worth continuing to pay for afterward.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the 10-inch screen a meaningful upgrade over the C 990's 7-inch screen?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, noticeably. The larger display makes trail video and workout content feel closer to watching on a small tablet than a phone, and stats are easier to read at a glance during faster-paced workouts. It's still smaller than the touchscreens on NordicTrack's Commercial-series machines, but it's a real step up from the C 990.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does assembly take?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Roughly 65 minutes working alone, similar to the C 990 since the frame and console are comparable in complexity despite the longer deck. A second person makes lifting the upright frame into place easier, but this remains a manageable solo project for most people with basic tools.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-06-03",
      dateModified: "2026-06-03",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Product",
      name: PRODUCT.name,
      description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: "NordicTrack" },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: PRODUCT.rating,
        reviewCount: PRODUCT.reviewCount,
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-06-03",
    modifiedTime: "2026-06-03",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "nordictrack c 1650",
      "nordictrack c series",
      "mid-range treadmill",
      "ifit treadmill",
      "home gym",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Check Price on Amazon", link: "#cta" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="relative bg-gray-900 overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-ntc1650" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-ntc1650)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="relative max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 lg:py-24 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Product Review
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated June 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight">
          NordicTrack <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">C 1650</span> Review
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          We put NordicTrack's mid-tier C-Series treadmill through four
          weeks of walking, jogging, and interval sessions to find out
          whether the longer deck and bigger screen make it the C-Series
          sweet spot.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#cta" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Check Price on Amazon
          </a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Jump to Verdict
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <!-- Author & Meta row -->
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">June 3, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="2.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Quick Verdict -->
      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-sm">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between flex-wrap gap-4 mb-4">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <div class="flex text-yellow-400">
              {[...Array(5)].map((_, i) => (
                <svg class={`w-5 h-5 ${i < Math.floor(PRODUCT.rating) ? "fill-current" : "text-gray-200"}`} xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor">
                  <path d="M9.049 2.927c.3-.921 1.603-.921 1.902 0l1.07 3.292a1 1 0 00.95.69h3.462c.969 0 1.371 1.24.588 1.81l-2.8 2.034a1 1 0 00-.364 1.118l1.07 3.292c.3.921-.755 1.688-1.54 1.118l-2.8-2.034a1 1 0 00-1.175 0l-2.8 2.034c-.784.57-1.838-.197-1.539-1.118l1.07-3.292a1 1 0 00-.364-1.118L2.98 8.72c-.783-.57-.38-1.81.588-1.81h3.461a1 1 0 00.951-.69l1.07-3.292z" />
                </svg>
              ))}
            </div>
            <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">{PRODUCT.rating}</span>
            <span class="text-gray-400 text-sm">({PRODUCT.reviewCount.toLocaleString()} reviews)</span>
          </div>
          <span class="bg-[#0F62FE]/10 text-[#0F62FE] px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold">Editor's Choice: C-Series Sweet Spot</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The C 1650 is the machine we'd point most C-Series shoppers
          toward. A 60-inch deck, matching NordicTrack's Commercial line in
          length, and a genuinely usable 10-inch touchscreen make it feel
          like meaningfully more treadmill than the C 990, without pushing
          all the way into Commercial-series pricing.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-4 pt-4 border-t border-gray-100">
          <p class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900">{PRODUCT.priceRange}</p>
          <a id="cta" href={PRODUCT.amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Check Price on Amazon &rarr;
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Table of Contents -->
      <div class="my-10 bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
        <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 mb-4">On This Page</p>
        <ol class="space-y-2 list-none p-0 m-0">
          <li><a href="#overview" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">1. Overview and Who It's For</a></li>
          <li><a href="#specs" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">2. Full Spec Sheet</a></li>
          <li><a href="#assembly" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">3. Assembly and Setup</a></li>
          <li><a href="#performance" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">4. Performance Deep Dive</a></li>
          <li><a href="#durability" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">5. Long-Term Durability Notes</a></li>
          <li><a href="#pros-cons" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">6. Pros and Cons</a></li>
          <li><a href="#comparison" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">7. How It Compares</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-should-skip" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">8. Who Should Skip This Treadmill</a></li>
          <li><a href="#verdict" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">9. Final Verdict</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a></li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="italic text-xl border-l-4 border-[#FF5A1F] bg-gray-50 pl-6 pr-4 py-4 rounded-r-xl not-prose text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
          NordicTrack's C-Series exists to answer a simple question: how
          much treadmill can you get without paying Commercial-series
          prices? The C 1650 is our answer to that question after a month
          of daily use — it's the model in the C-Series lineup that stops
          feeling like a compromise and starts feeling like a genuinely
          well-rounded home treadmill.
        </p>

        <h2 id="overview" class="scroll-mt-24">Overview and Who It's For</h2>
        <p>
          The C 1650 sits above the C 990 in NordicTrack's entry-level
          lineup, and the gap between them is smaller in price than it is
          in usable deck length. We tested it specifically against the
          question shoppers ask us most when comparing these two models:
          is the roughly $300 jump from the C 990 actually worth it, or is
          it just a bigger number on the spec sheet? After four weeks, our
          answer is that the extra five inches of deck length is genuinely
          noticeable in daily use, particularly for anyone who jogs rather
          than only walks, and the larger touchscreen is a real quality-of-
          life upgrade rather than a marketing footnote.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's worth placing the C 1650 accurately within NordicTrack's
          broader lineup before you buy. It's still part of the C-Series,
          positioned below the Commercial-series machines like the
          <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill">Commercial 1750</a>
          and well below the X-Series incline trainers. The C 1650's 20 x
          60-inch deck actually matches the length of the Commercial
          1750's deck, though it's two inches narrower and paired with a
          less powerful 3.0 CHP motor rather than the Commercial line's
          3.75 CHP. That's a meaningfully different value proposition than
          the C 990, which falls short of Commercial-series deck length in
          both directions.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is a folding treadmill using NordicTrack's SpaceSaver design
          with an EasyLift assist strut. Standing folded, it occupies a
          35 x 76-inch footprint — larger than the C 990 given the extra
          deck length, but still meaningfully more compact than the
          Commercial-series machines. It's a reasonable middle ground for
          households that want more usable running space than the C 990
          offers without committing to the larger footprint of a
          Commercial-tier machine.
        </p>
        <p>
          We'd recommend the C 1650 most strongly to a household that walks
          regularly and jogs at least a few times a week, where the extra
          deck length actually gets used, and to anyone upgrading from a
          cheaper or older treadmill who wants a noticeably better screen
          experience without paying Commercial-series prices. If you're
          purely a walker who never plans to jog, the money might be better
          saved by sticking with the <a href="/nordictrack-c-990-treadmill">C 990</a>;
          if you're a serious runner logging consistent faster-paced miles,
          the Commercial 1750's stronger motor and wider deck are worth the
          additional investment.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8 my-8">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-6 text-center">
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">3.0</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">CHP Motor</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">20x60</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Deck (inches)</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">0/10%</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Max Incline</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">300</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Lbs. Capacity</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="specs" class="scroll-mt-24">Full Spec Sheet</h2>
        <p>
          Here's every spec we verified directly against our test unit
          rather than copying from the manufacturer's marketing page.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8 rounded-xl border border-gray-200">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <tbody>
            {SPECS.map((spec, i) => (
              <tr class={i < SPECS.length - 1 ? "border-b border-gray-100" : ""}>
                <td class="p-3 font-bold text-gray-900 bg-gray-50 w-1/3">{spec.label}</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">{spec.value}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <h2 id="assembly" class="scroll-mt-24">Assembly and Setup</h2>
        <p>
          Assembly took our team roughly 65 minutes working alone, similar
          to the C 990 despite the longer deck, since the console and frame
          complexity are comparable across the C-Series. A second person
          still makes lifting the upright frame into place noticeably
          easier, and the manual recommends it, but this remains a
          manageable solo project for most people with basic tools and
          about an hour of free time.
        </p>
        <p>
          The console arrives mostly pre-wired, and the included tools
          were sufficient for the entire build. The instructions were
          clear and the hardware bags matched the manual's step numbering
          closely enough that we never had to guess which bolt went where.
          The box is somewhat heavier than the C 990's given the longer
          deck rails, closer to 200 pounds, so plan your delivery and
          unboxing location accordingly.
        </p>
        <p>
          As with every treadmill we test, we'd recommend a short
          calibration pass before your first real workout: run the machine
          through its full 0-10% incline range once, listen for any
          unusual noise, and confirm the safety key stops the belt
          immediately when pulled. It's a five-minute habit that catches
          rare shipping-damage issues early.
        </p>

        <h2 id="performance" class="scroll-mt-24">Performance Deep Dive</h2>
        <h3>Motor and Deck</h3>
        <p>
          The 3.0 CHP motor is identical to the one in the C 990, and it
          performed the same way here — comfortable and unstrained during
          walking and moderate jogging, with more audible effort showing up
          during sustained running above roughly 7 mph. What's different is
          the deck: at 20 x 60 inches, the C 1650 matches the Commercial
          1750's deck length, and the difference versus the C 990's 55-inch
          deck was immediately noticeable to our taller testers. Runners
          over 6 feet who felt cramped on the C 990 reported feeling
          comfortable at full stride on the C 1650, which is the single
          biggest practical upgrade this model offers over its cheaper
          sibling.
        </p>
        <p>
          We ran the same heat-soak protocol we use on every treadmill: 45
          minutes of continuous activity, checking motor housing
          temperature by hand every ten minutes, capped at 6 mph given the
          shared motor's intended use case. The housing warmed steadily but
          stayed comfortable to the touch throughout, and belt speed held
          consistent through the test. Belt tracking stayed centered
          without drifting, matching what we saw on the C 990 and
          suggesting consistent quality control across the C-Series line.
        </p>
        <h3>Incline and the iFit Experience</h3>
        <p>
          The 0-10% incline range matches the C 990 exactly, and it remains
          a genuinely useful range for incline walking rather than a token
          gesture toward the feature. Automatic incline adjustments during
          iFit-guided workouts responded smoothly, and the included
          first-year iFit membership gives new users time to evaluate
          whether the coached format is worth continuing to pay for. The
          experience here is functionally identical to the C 990's, which
          makes sense given the shared incline hardware — the differences
          between these two models live in the deck and the screen, not
          the incline system.
        </p>
        <h3>The 10-Inch Touchscreen</h3>
        <p>
          This is the other major upgrade over the C 990, and it's a
          meaningful one. The 10-inch display made trail video and workout
          content noticeably easier to engage with, closer to watching on a
          small tablet than the C 990's more phone-sized 7-inch screen.
          Reading stats at a glance during faster-paced workouts was
          easier too, since text and numbers render larger without feeling
          cramped. It's still a step below the touchscreens on NordicTrack's
          Commercial-series machines, but the jump from the C 990 is real
          and, in our testing, was the upgrade our testers commented on
          unprompted most often.
        </p>
        <h3>Console, Sound, and Everyday Usability</h3>
        <p>
          Outside of guided workouts, the console handles the basics well,
          with physical buttons on the handrail for speed and incline so
          you don't need to touch the screen mid-workout to make small
          adjustments. The built-in speakers are the same size as the C
          990's and correspondingly modest, fine for a small to
          medium-sized room but not designed to fill a larger space
          without help from a separate speaker. Bluetooth pairing was
          reliable throughout testing.
        </p>
        <h3>Folding and Footprint</h3>
        <p>
          The EasyLift assist strut works as expected, and while the C
          1650 is somewhat heavier than the C 990 given its longer deck,
          folding still felt manageable for a single person. It's not
          quite as effortless as folding the C 990, but it's considerably
          easier than folding a Commercial-series machine, striking a
          reasonable middle ground that matches its positioning in the
          lineup.
        </p>

        <h3>Cushioning Compared to the Commercial Line</h3>
        <p>
          Like the C 990, the C 1650 uses NordicTrack's standard FlexSelect
          cushioning rather than the adjustable multi-setting version found
          on the Commercial-series machines, so there's no lever to toggle
          firmness for different workout types. In practice, the fixed
          tuning felt slightly firmer underfoot than we expected walking in
          after testing the Commercial 1750, though not uncomfortably so —
          it's a reasonable middle ground that favors stability at jogging
          paces over plush softness during long walks. Testers who moved
          directly from the C 990 to the C 1650 didn't notice a meaningful
          difference in cushioning feel between the two, which suggests the
          cushioning tuning is consistent across the C-Series rather than
          scaling with price the way deck length and screen size do.
        </p>
        <h3>A Maintenance Tip Worth Following</h3>
        <p>
          Because the C 1650 shares its motor with the smaller C 990, the
          same maintenance advice applies here: don't treat the
          manufacturer's belt lubrication schedule as optional. The longer
          60-inch deck means more belt surface area in play, and we'd
          specifically recommend checking belt tension a little more
          frequently than you might on a shorter deck, since a longer belt
          run gives slightly more room for drift before it becomes
          noticeable underfoot. A few minutes of maintenance every couple
          of months goes a long way toward keeping the motor from working
          harder than it needs to.
        </p>

        <h2 id="durability" class="scroll-mt-24">Long-Term Durability Notes</h2>
        <p>
          Four weeks of testing can't fully answer the multi-year
          durability question, so we cross-referenced our hands-on
          impressions against patterns in verified owner reviews spanning
          one to three years of ownership. Belt tracking and tension came
          up as the most common maintenance item, consistent with what we
          found on the C 990 and with treadmills generally. Owners who
          used the C 1650 primarily for running reported the shared 3.0 CHP
          motor showing more audible effort over time than NordicTrack's
          Commercial-series machines, though complaint rates specific to
          motor failure were low. The longer deck itself drew positive
          long-term feedback, with several owners specifically noting it
          held up well without developing the sag or unevenness that can
          show up on cheaper decks after a year or more of regular use.
        </p>
        <p>
          NordicTrack's warranty coverage — a 10-year frame, 2-year parts,
          and 1-year labor policy — matches the rest of the lineup,
          including the pricier Commercial-series machines, which is a
          genuinely strong warranty for a treadmill in this price range.
          We'd recommend registering your unit promptly and keeping the
          belt lubricated on the manufacturer's suggested schedule; as with
          the C 990, this motor has less headroom than the Commercial
          line's, so skipped maintenance is likely to show up as strain
          sooner than it would on a more powerful machine.
        </p>

