      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The <a href="/nordictrack-c-990-treadmill">C 990</a> is the
          natural comparison if you want to save more — it shares the C
          1650's motor and incline range but trades five inches of deck
          length and a smaller screen for a roughly $300 lower price. If
          you're purely a walker with no plans to run, that trade-off may
          not cost you anything in practice. The
          <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill">Commercial 1750</a>
          is the comparison worth making if you want more headroom: a
          stronger 3.75 CHP motor, a wider 22-inch deck, and a considerably
          larger touchscreen, for roughly $500 more than the C 1650.
        </p>
        <p>
          Positioned between those two, the C 1650 earns its label as the
          C-Series sweet spot. It closes most of the practical gap with the
          Commercial 1750 on deck length while staying meaningfully cheaper,
          and it closes the screen-size gap with the C 990 while adding
          real usable running space. For a large share of shoppers who
          don't need the Commercial line's full motor headroom, this is
          where the value curve peaks in NordicTrack's current lineup.
        </p>

