          <p class="text-amber-800 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            NordicTrack runs frequent sales, especially around major
            holidays. If you're not in a rush, watch the price for a few
            weeks — it's common to see the C 1650 drop $150-$250 below its
            typical street price during promotional periods, narrowing the
            gap to the C 990 considerably.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">Who Should Skip This Treadmill</h2>
        <p>
          As much as we like the C 1650, it isn't the right machine for
          every shopper. If you're a purely dedicated walker who will never
          use the extra deck length, the
          <a href="/nordictrack-c-990-treadmill">C 990</a> saves you real
          money without costing you anything you'd actually use. If you're
          a serious runner logging consistent faster-paced miles, the
          narrower deck and lower-powered motor relative to the
          <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill">Commercial 1750</a>
          will start to feel limiting, and the extra investment there is
          worth it. And if you know for certain you'll never pay for an
          iFit subscription, you're still buying into hardware built around
          that ecosystem, so it's worth weighing a simpler non-connected
          treadmill from our
          <a href="/best-treadmill-for-home-reviews">home treadmill guide</a>
          instead.
        </p>

        <h2 id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">Final Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          The NordicTrack C 1650 is the C-Series model we'd actually
          recommend buying for most households. The 60-inch deck removes
          the cramped feeling taller runners reported on the C 990, and the
          10-inch touchscreen makes the iFit experience feel considerably
          less like an entry-level afterthought. It's still built around a
          3.0 CHP motor that shows its limits during sustained faster
          running, and the iFit dependency for full functionality remains a
          real consideration, but as an all-around home treadmill for
          walking, incline walking, and moderate jogging, it's a
          genuinely well-balanced machine.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you've read this far and you're weighing the C 1650 against
          its siblings, our advice is straightforward: buy based on how
          you'll actually train. Pure walkers can save money with the C
          990 and lose little. Serious runners should keep saving toward
          the Commercial 1750. Everyone in between — which describes most
          households — will find the C 1650 delivers the deck length and
          screen quality to make daily use genuinely pleasant without
          overspending.
        </p>
